page('mappingintro',head('CURRICULUM LINEAGE','Read the crosswalk carefully')+para('The accessible official Primary 1–3 Mathematics body has 37 pages. Its companion introduction identifies the revision as 2012 on printed page iv (PDF page 4). The body’s printed and PDF page numbers match. This book’s register contains 155 individually paraphrased performance objectives: 54 for Primary 1, 40 for Primary 2 and 61 for Primary 3.')+para('The crosswalk records source objective IDs, lesson evidence and assessment IDs. It is a historical authoring crosswalk. It does not establish current NERDC alignment or official approval. Supplementary teacher assessments check skills that need practical or oral observation. Recognition of real coins/notes depends on verified adult-supplied examples.')+para('A newer national curriculum was announced in September 2025. The official revised class-level links returned 403, 403 and 404 for Primary 1, 2 and 3 during this research. The applicable revised Mathematics contents and Gombe adoption remain unverified.')+para('Acquisition route: request the September 2025 Mathematics Primary 1, 2 and 3 volumes, publication identifiers, teacher guides and implementation notice from NERDC: info@nerdc.gov.ng; +2347081361390. Its official bookshop page lists headquarters at Sheda, FCT and a Northeast outlet at Yobe Government Secretariat, Damaturu. Confirm stock and Gombe SUBEB adoption before relying on these editions.')+note('Source issues','The legacy addition boundary differs between objective and content columns; we use total ≤18. A factor example for 30 is corrected. Primary 3 Length II is retained through standard-measure lessons. Money examples are explicitly historical pretend values. Full evidence and page notes are in research/CURRICULUM.md.'))
